<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Halidon Hill</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1333">1333</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-war-of-scottish-independence">Second War of Scottish Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Halidon Hill was fought on 19 July 1333 outside Berwick, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-balliol">Edward Balliol</a> defeated a Scottish army under the regent Archibald Douglas that was trying to relieve the besieged town. English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> inflicted heavy losses, Douglas was killed and Berwick surrendered. The defeat forced the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> II into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1332 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-balliol">Edward Balliol</a>, son of King John I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, led the Scottish nobles disinherited after the Treaty of Northampton in an invasion of Scotland, quietly supported by Edward III. He crushed a Scottish army at Dupplin Moor and was crowned at Scone, granting Edward lands that included Berwick, but supporters of the five-year-old David II ambushed him at Annan in December and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Edward then openly recognised Balliol and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a> as his target: the most successful trading town in Scotland, whose wool duties were the Crown's largest source of income, and a place the Scots could hardly abandon.</p><p>Balliol reached Berwick in late March 1333 and Edward joined him on 9 May. The guardian, Sir Archibald Douglas, gathered an army that the historian Clifford Rogers puts at around 15,000. When the town's governor, Sir Alexander Seton, broke a truce, Edward hanged his son Thomas, a hostage, in sight of the walls; a new truce required the town to surrender if not relieved by sunset on 19 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Edward placed his army on Halidon Hill, a rise of some 600 feet north-west of Berwick, with dismounted men-at-arms and spearmen in three divisions and longbowmen on each flank. After a stalemate Douglas ordered an assault a little after noon. The Scots had to cross marshy ground and climb the slope under dense arrow fire, and arrived tired, having marched 60 miles in 48 hours. Two of their three schiltrons broke almost at once; the third fought on before it too collapsed.</p><p>English men-at-arms mounted and pursued the fleeing Scots for 8 miles. Most who surrendered were killed on Edward's orders, and about a hundred prisoners were beheaded the next morning. English chronicles claimed 35,000 to 60,000 Scottish dead and Scottish ones 10,000; Rogers suggests about 2,900. Six of the nine most senior Scots present died, while English losses were reported as 14 or 7.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Berwick surrendered on 20 July. Balliol held a parliament at Perth in October 1333 and in 1334 ceded eight counties of south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a>, but he was deposed, restored and deposed again by 1336. The outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> in 1337 let David II consolidate his position. The battlefield is on Historic England's Register of Historic Battlefields, and a rough stone monument stands near the top of the hill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Halidon Hill” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Second War of Independence brought defeats at <b>Halidon Hill</b> in 1333 and Neville’s Cross in 1346, where David II was captured.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li></ul></section>
